If chatGPT is a tool that provides information that has been aggregated/collected from people, why should the enterprize put a pricing to it.

If by “ChatGPT” you mean an enterprise product built on information ultimately produced by people, the key distinction is between the information itself and the service that makes that information useful at scale.

An enterprise can reasonably charge for ChatGPT because it is not simply selling a collection of people's information. It is selling a combination of:

Compute: Generating an answer requires substantial GPU/CPU resources, especially for large models.
Model development: Training and improving models requires expensive research, engineering, data processing, evaluation, and experimentation.
Infrastructure: Enterprises need servers, storage, networking, security, backups, monitoring, and reliability.
Product/interface: The model is packaged into software that employees can actually use—search, analysis, writing, coding, document processing, integrations, etc.
Enterprise controls: Companies pay for things like access management, auditability, compliance, data controls, administration, and support.
Convenience and productivity: Instead of an employee spending two hours searching, reading, synthesizing, and drafting something, the system may reduce that to minutes. The price can be justified by the economic value created.


But your question points to a very important philosophical and economic issue:

If the underlying knowledge was created by society, why should a private company be able to put a price on access to it?

That's a legitimate question. There are actually several layers here.

1. Information isn't necessarily the same thing as the service

Suppose thousands of people have written publicly about accounting, programming, medicine, history, etc. An AI company doesn't necessarily own those underlying facts.

What it has built is a system that can compress, organize, retrieve, reason over, and generate responses from patterns learned from enormous amounts of information.

It's similar to a search engine. The web existed before Google, but Google could charge businesses for advertising and enterprise services because building the infrastructure to index and retrieve it has economic value.

2. But AI creates a new ownership question

This is where your argument becomes stronger.

Traditional software is often based on something the company created itself. AI models are different because their capabilities emerge partly from human-created knowledge and culture.

So there is a legitimate debate over:

Should creators whose work contributed to training receive compensation?
Should AI companies be allowed to commercially exploit publicly available content?
Should models trained on public knowledge be treated partly like public infrastructure?
How much of the price represents genuine technological cost versus economic rent?
Should governments or institutions provide publicly funded AI models?

There isn't a universally accepted answer.

3. Enterprise pricing isn't necessarily charging for "knowledge"

Think about an employee asking:

"Analyze these 500 pages of documents and give me the key risks."

The company isn't really paying for the fact that the information exists. It's paying for the computational system that processes those documents and produces the analysis.

The same principle applies to enterprise databases, cloud computing, search engines, analytics software, etc.

4. There is also a business reality

Even if all the underlying information were freely available, operating an AI system at enterprise scale isn't free.

If a company has 100,000 employees using an AI system continuously, somebody has to pay for:

chips → data centers → electricity → networking → engineers → researchers → security → storage → maintenance → support

Enterprise pricing is one mechanism for recovering those costs and funding further development.

The more interesting question

I think your question becomes particularly interesting if you phrase it this way:

"Should AI companies be allowed to privately monetize intelligence derived from humanity's collective knowledge?"

That's much harder to answer.

One possible future is today's model: private companies build AI and sell access to it.

Another possibility is a model analogous to public infrastructure: governments, universities, and nonprofits collectively fund open/public AI models, while private companies compete on top of them.

And there could be a hybrid model where AI companies pay creators/data providers, while users ultimately pay for the computation and services.

So I wouldn't say "ChatGPT deserves to have a price because it contains valuable information." I'd say:

The information may be largely humanity's collective creation, but the infrastructure and technology for turning that information into an interactive, scalable service have real costs and economic value. Whether the resulting profits are fairly distributed is a separate—and very legitimate—question.

I am not here to argue pricing since there is already a free version provided which to me is fair deal.

Exactly. And I think that makes your original question more nuanced.

You’re not objecting to pricing; you're questioning why a privately owned enterprise should be the entity providing and monetizing something whose intellectual foundation is largely collective human knowledge.

The free tier changes the discussion because it creates a kind of social bargain:

Humanity contributes the accumulated knowledge and culture. The company builds the machinery that makes that knowledge interactively useful. The company gives people some level of access for free, while charging organizations that need much greater scale, reliability, controls, and support.

That can be viewed as reasonably fair.

There is also an interesting distinction between information and intelligence-as-a-service. A book may contain knowledge for free in a library, but having someone—or an AI—read 10,000 books, synthesize them, answer questions about them instantly, and work with you interactively is a different service.

So perhaps the more fundamental question isn't:

"Why should ChatGPT cost money?"

but:

"What is the appropriate relationship between collectively created human knowledge and privately developed AI systems that learn from it?"

And that question becomes increasingly important as AI becomes less like a search tool and more like a general-purpose layer between humans and knowledge.

argument accepted. Give me  a command using curl to post a request to chatGPT and recieve response on terminal itself

Yes. If you want to call the OpenAI API directly from a terminal, the current approach is the Responses API. OpenAI's current model documentation lists GPT-5.6 Luna as a cost-sensitive model.

First set your API key:

export OPENAI_API_KEY="your_api_key_here"

Then:

curl https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  -d '{
    "model": "gpt-5.6-luna",
    "input": "Explain quantum computing in simple terms."
  }'

That returns JSON containing the model's response.

If you want only the answer on the terminal

Using jq makes it much cleaner:

curl -s https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  -d '{
    "model": "gpt-5.6-luna",
    "input": "Explain quantum computing in simple terms."
  }' | jq -r '.output[]?.content[]?.text // empty'

You can then make it interactive:

read -p "You: " prompt

curl -s https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  -d "$(jq -n --arg p "$prompt" '{
    model: "gpt-5.6-luna",
    input: $p
  }')" | jq -r '.output[]?.content[]?.text // empty'

So you could literally have:

$ read -p "You: " prompt
You: What is the difference between TCP and UDP?

TCP is connection-oriented...

Important: ChatGPT's consumer subscription and the OpenAI API are separate products/billing systems. An API key is required for API calls; having access to ChatGPT itself doesn't automatically mean API usage is included.

OpenAI API documentation

when we assume cost sensitive factor, can you please tell how many requests are permitted within limit.

Yes—but there’s an important distinction: API limits aren't usually expressed as a simple “N requests per day.” They depend on your API usage tier and the particular model. OpenAI applies rate limits such as requests per minute (RPM) and tokens per minute (TPM).

Also, I should correct something from my previous answer: “GPT-5.6 Luna” is not an API model name I can verify from the official API documentation, so you shouldn't use that model name in your curl command without checking the models available to your API account.

For a cost-sensitive setup, the important calculation is:

requests you can make ≈ TPM limit ÷ average tokens consumed per request

For example, if your account had a hypothetical 100,000 TPM limit and each request consumed ~1,000 tokens:

100,000 ÷ 1,000 ≈ 100 requests/minute

But if each request consumes 5,000 tokens:

100,000 ÷ 5,000 ≈ 20 requests/minute

So the number of requests isn't fixed independently of the amount of text you're sending/receiving.

If you tell me which API model you're planning to use (for example, a specific GPT-5.x model), I can look up its current price + RPM/TPM limits and calculate roughly how many terminal curl questions you could afford per $1, $5, or $10.

whats the limit under free account

If you mean a free ChatGPT account, that's different from a free API account.

ChatGPT Free

The free ChatGPT service currently has a usage limit rather than a fixed number of API-style requests. OpenAI says Free users get limited access to its main model within a time window, and ChatGPT tells you when you've reached the limit and when access resets.

But for your curl experiment

This is the important part: your free ChatGPT account does not give you a corresponding free API allowance.

The API is separately metered. In other words:

ChatGPT Free account
        │
        └── Free use through chatgpt.com

OpenAI API
        │
        └── Separate billing/credits
             └── curl → /v1/responses

So if your goal is:

curl ... /v1/responses

you should not assume that having ChatGPT Free gives you, say, 100 or 1,000 free API calls. Current OpenAI API documentation treats rate limits according to API usage tiers, and some API models have no free tier at all.

There have historically been free/trial API credits, but I would not rely on those being available to a newly created account; OpenAI's current information is account/eligibility dependent.

One correction to my earlier answer: I shouldn't have implied that you could simply use a normal

ChatGPT Free account to make API curl calls for free.

If your objective is simply "I want to experiment with curl and make perhaps 50–200 text questions without spending much", I can show you the cheapest currently available OpenAI API setup and estimate the cost per 100 questions.

yes, show me the estimate

Sure. I checked the current OpenAI API pricing because these numbers change.

For a cheap terminal-based chatbot, I'd look at GPT-5 nano first, or GPT-5.4 mini if you want noticeably stronger answers. OpenAI currently lists GPT-5 nano at $0.05 / 1M input tokens and $0.40 / 1M output tokens. GPT-5.4 mini is $0.75 / 1M input and $4.50 / 1M output.

What does 100 questions cost?

Let's assume each question/answer exchange uses approximately:

500 input tokens — your question + instructions/context

500 output tokens — AI's answer

That's 1,000 tokens per interaction.

Model	100 interactions	1,000 interactions	10,000 interactions
GPT-5 nano	~$0.023	~$0.225	~$2.25
GPT-5.4 mini	~$0.263	~$2.63	~$26.25
GPT-5	~$0.563	~$5.63	~$56.25

These calculations use the current per-token prices published by OpenAI. GPT-5, for example, is $1.25/M input and $10/M output.

So for your experimentation:

100 ordinary questions could cost only a few cents with GPT-5 nano.

Even 1,000 questions could be around 22–23 cents under those assumptions.

But here's the catch

The actual cost depends heavily on output length.

For example, if you ask:

What is TCP?

and get:

TCP is a connection-oriented protocol...

that's tiny.

But if you ask:

Explain TCP comprehensively, compare it with UDP,
give examples, discuss packet structure and reliability...

and receive a 2,000-token answer, your cost increases accordingly.

Also, reasoning models can consume additional internal reasoning tokens, depending on the model/settings.

What I'd use for your curl experiment

I'd start with GPT-5 nano if your objective is simply learning how the API works cheaply.

For example:

curl -s https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $OPENAI_API_KEY" \
  -d '{
    "model": "gpt-5-nano",
    "input": "Explain TCP versus UDP in five sentences."
  }'

One correction to my earlier response is worth emphasizing: you don't need to use GPT-5.6 Luna for this experiment. OpenAI's current API documentation actually recommends GPT-5.6 Luna for cost-sensitive

workloads, but the exact model availability/pricing should be checked against your API account.

If you're mainly experimenting from the shell, $1 of API credit can go surprisingly far.

And if you want, I can give you a 10-line Bash script that turns curl into a proper interactive ChatGPT-like terminal chat, where you repeatedly type questions and it maintains the conversation history.

Then let's make your terminal feel like a tiny ChatGPT. 😄

This version keeps conversation history, so you can ask follow-up questions naturally:

#!/usr/bin/env bash

MODEL="gpt-5-nano"
PREVIOUS_RESPONSE=""

while true; do
    printf "\nYou: "
    read -r prompt

    [[ "$prompt" == "exit" ]] && break
    [[ -z "$prompt" ]] && continue

    if [[ -n "$PREVIOUS_RESPONSE" ]]; then
        BODY=$(jq -n \
            --arg model "$MODEL" \
            --arg input "$prompt" \
            --arg previous "$PREVIOUS_RESPONSE" \
            '{
                model: $model,
                previous_response_id: $previous,
                input: $input
            }')
    else
        BODY=$(jq -n \
            --arg model "$MODEL" \
            --arg input "$prompt" \
            '{
                model: $model,
                input: $input
            }')
    fi

    RESPONSE=$(curl -s https://api.openai.com/v1/responses \
        -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
        -H "Authorization: Bearer '"$OPENAI_API_KEY"'" \
        -d "$BODY")

    echo "$RESPONSE" | jq -r '.output[]?.content[]?.text // empty'

    PREVIOUS_RESPONSE=$(echo "$RESPONSE" | jq -r '.id // empty')
done

Save it as:

nano chat.sh

Make it executable:

chmod +x chat.sh

Then:

./chat.sh

You can have a conversation like:

You: My name is Raj.

Hello Raj! Nice to meet you.

You: What is my name?

Your name is Raj.

You: What did I tell you about myself?

You told me your name is Raj.

And when you've had enough:

You: exit
The really interesting part

Notice this:

previous_response_id

We're not sending the entire conversation back to the API every time. We're telling the Responses API which response this new question follows.

That gives you the beginnings of a real conversational agent in roughly 30 lines of Bash.

You can take this surprisingly far—stream responses as they're generated, add colors, pipe files into it, ask it to analyze logs, make it read stdin, or turn it into a Linux command-line assistant.

If you want the next-level version, I can give you one that streams the answer onto your terminal word-by-word, like ChatGPT is actually typing in front of you.

